<div class="wrap page-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">Services / 01</span><h1>A new chapter.<br>A familiar place.</h1></div></div><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/services/' ) ); ?>">All services</a> / Renovations &amp; additions</div><div class="detail-hero"><div><p>Good renovation begins with understanding what deserves to stay. We look for the smallest set of changes that can make a home work better: a clearer plan, a stronger garden connection, a little more daylight in the right place.</p><p>From reworking a single floor to a measured addition, the process brings your daily needs, the building’s condition, and the available budget into the same conversation.</p><a class="button" href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/contact/' ) ); ?>">Talk about your renovation<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><figure class=""><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_theme_file_uri( '/assets/images/portfolio/editorial-detail.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Weathered beige walls with an arched recess and a tall opening to the sky" width="1600" height="1086" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption class="caption"><span>Architectural reference · illustrative photography</span><span>Annie Spratt / Unsplash</span></figcaption></figure></div></div>
<section class="wash section"><div class="wrap"><span class="eyebrow">The scope / What we consider</span><h2>See the whole.<br>Resolve the details.</h2><div class="scope-list"><article><h3>Layout &amp; everyday use</h3><p>Map how you move through the home, where belongings gather, and which rooms need privacy or connection. Compare options before adding area.</p></article><article><h3>Light &amp; comfort</h3><p>Consider orientation, shading, ventilation opportunities, and the depth of the plan. Coordinate specialist input where performance needs closer investigation.</p></article><article><h3>Character &amp; materials</h3><p>Identify elements worth retaining and assess how old and new can meet. Develop finishes and joinery with maintenance and repair in mind.</p></article><article><h3>Buildability &amp; sequence</h3><p>Review structural changes, service routes, site access, and possible phases with the relevant consultants and builder. Make the work understandable before it begins.</p></article></div><p class="fine-print">A project proposal would define the exact appointment. Surveys, engineering, statutory fees, and construction costs are separate items to discuss.</p></div></section>
<section class="wrap section"><span class="eyebrow">The process</span><h2>One clear decision at a time.</h2><ol class="process-list"><li><span class="number">01</span><h3>Understand the existing</h3><p>Gather your brief, available drawings, and a condition overview. Identify the measured survey and specialist investigations needed to make informed decisions.</p></li><li><span class="number">02</span><h3>Test the possibilities</h3><p>Explore a small number of layout options, including what could be achieved within the current footprint. Discuss priorities and seek cost input early.</p></li><li><span class="number">03</span><h3>Develop the design</h3><p>Resolve the chosen arrangement, material direction, and key details. Coordinate consultant input and identify the relevant approval route for the site.</p></li><li><span class="number">04</span><h3>Prepare for building</h3><p>Produce the agreed drawings and specifications for pricing and construction. Confirm responsibilities, information needs, and the scope of any site-stage support.</p></li></ol></section>
<section class="section wash"><div class="wrap example-strip"><figure class="landscape-photo"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_theme_file_uri( '/assets/images/hero/editorial-hero.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="White concrete facade with long horizontal windows beneath a pale blue sky" width="1600" height="1067" loading="lazy"><figcaption class="caption"><span>Architectural reference · illustrative photography</span><span>Pierre Châtel-Innocenti / Unsplash</span></figcaption></figure><div><span class="eyebrow">An example / Fictional concept</span><h2>Courtyard House</h2><p>A narrow house becomes easier to live in by moving circulation toward a small courtyard. The front rooms remain intact, while the shared kitchen and dining space gain a direct relationship with the garden.</p><p>The lesson is simple: a change in the plan can matter more than a change in size.</p><a class="text-link" href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/work/#courtyard-house' ) ); ?>">Read the project study<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div></section>
<section class="wrap section faq-layout"><div><span class="eyebrow">Renovation questions</span><h2>A little clarity<br>before you start.</h2></div><div><details><summary>Can we live at home during the work?</summary><p>Sometimes, but it depends on access, services, dust, noise, and the extent of structural work. Discuss this with the design team and builder early. A phased plan may help, while some projects need a temporary move.</p></details><details><summary>Will we need planning permission?</summary><p>That depends on the location, property, and proposed changes. The relevant local requirements would need to be checked for your actual project. This fictional sample cannot determine an approval route.</p></details><details><summary>What if our budget is still uncertain?</summary><p>Start with a comfortable investment range and a list of priorities. Early options can then be tested with appropriate cost advice. Keep professional fees, approvals, contingency, and temporary accommodation in the overall picture.</p></details><details><summary>Can you guarantee a completion date?</summary><p>No date can be established from an initial idea alone. Surveys, approvals, design decisions, contractor availability, and the condition of the existing building all affect the schedule. A realistic program would be developed as those unknowns become clearer.</p></details></div></section><?php get_template_part( 'template-parts/inquiry' ); ?>
